# Swift Concurrency

## Actor isolation

Declare isolation on the type. Do not patch it at the call site.

```swift
// ❌ BAD - UI mutated from an arbitrary executor, then hand-hopped
func load() async {
    let items = await api.fetch()
    DispatchQueue.main.async { self.items = items }
}

// ✅ GOOD - isolation is part of the type's contract
@MainActor
final class FeedViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published private(set) var items: [Item] = []

    func load() async {
        items = await api.fetch()
    }
}
```

Annotate view models and any type that publishes UI state with `@MainActor`.

## Structured concurrency

- Prefer `async let` and `TaskGroup` over `DispatchGroup` or nested completion handlers.
- Store any `Task` you start from a view or controller, and cancel it on teardown.
- Never block waiting on async work. `semaphore.wait()` and `DispatchQueue.sync` from an async context can deadlock the cooperative thread pool.

## Sendable

- Types crossing an actor boundary must be `Sendable`. Prefer value types.
- Use `@unchecked Sendable` only when the type is genuinely immutable or guards its own state with a lock, and add a comment naming the invariant that makes it safe.

## Reentrancy

An actor can process other messages while suspended. After every `await` inside an actor, re-read any state you captured before the suspension instead of assuming it is unchanged.
